# Cyrius Field Notes — Language Gotchas (semantics & runtime)
# Var lifetime + scope, struct locals, init order, fn main, static `var buf[N]`
# inside library fns, the allocator's process-lifetime persistence + arena
# exhaustion policy, i64::MIN arithmetic, and thread handle lifetime.
# Refreshed 2026-08-07 at v6.5.10: the global bump allocator has CHAINED
# CHUNKS since v6.1.19 (measured — 400 MB in 100 MB bites succeeds), so the
# old "exhaust the heap segment in 9 allocs" story no longer describes it;
# three entries added from v6.5.8/.9.
# 16 entries.

[[entries]]
name = "heap_allocation_patterns"
description = "How to structure heap-allocated data in a language without structs-on-heap"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius has structs, but for heap data you typically use raw alloc + store64/load64.
The pattern that emerged across both projects:

1. POINTER + ACCESSOR FUNCTIONS:

   var map_nodes = 0;
   fn map_alloc() { map_nodes = alloc(1024 * 112); }
   fn map_node_x(idx) { return load64(map_nodes + idx * 112); }
   fn map_node_y(idx) { return load64(map_nodes + idx * 112 + 8); }

   This is essentially manual vtable-free OOP. The accessor functions ARE
   your type safety. Name them consistently: module_field(index).

2. ENUM FOR LAYOUT CONSTANTS:

   enum BspNode {
       BSP_NODE_SIZE = 112;
       BSP_NODE_X = 0;
       BSP_NODE_Y = 8;
       BSP_NODE_DX = 16;
   }

   Enums cost zero gvar_toks. Use them for every struct layout.

3. LAZY INIT GUARD:

   fn framebuf_set_palette(data) {
       if (palette == 0) { palette = alloc(768); }
       memcpy(palette, data, 768);
   }

   We found a real segfault from this: framebuf_set_palette() was called
   before framebuf_init() allocated the buffer. The lazy guard fixed it.
   Always guard heap pointers that might be called before init.

4. PACK SMALL STATE:

   # Instead of 3 globals:
   var player_start = 0;
   fn map_find_player_start() {
       if (player_start == 0) { player_start = alloc(24); }
       store64(player_start, x);
       store64(player_start + 8, y);
       store64(player_start + 16, angle);
   }
   fn player_start_x() { return load64(player_start); }

[[entries]]
name = "enum_as_namespace"
description = "Using enums as zero-cost namespaced constants"
category = "cyrius"
---
Enums in Cyrius are compile-time integer constants. They don't consume
gvar_toks slots — they const-fold, and only NON-literal top-level
`var X = expr;` inits take a gvar_toks slot at all. (This line used to
say "a separate initialization path in cc2"; `cc2` has not been the
compiler since the v3.x era — the current one is `cycc`. See
`diagnostics_caps.cyml::the_gvar_wall` for the live table.)

This makes them perfect for constants that would otherwise eat your
global variable budget:

  # BAD: 8 gvar_toks slots
  var MAP_MAX_VERTEXES = 2048;
  var MAP_MAX_LINEDEFS = 2048;
  var MAP_MAX_SIDEDEFS = 2048;
  ...

  # GOOD: 0 gvar_toks slots
  enum MapMax {
      MAP_MAX_VERTEXES = 2048;
      MAP_MAX_LINEDEFS = 2048;
      MAP_MAX_SIDEDEFS = 2048;
      ...
  }

Both produce identical code — the values are inlined at use sites.

WARNING: Enum VALUES must be non-negative integers — `enum E { NEG = -1; }`
still errors 'expected number, got -' (verified v6.4.2). For a negative
constant, declare it as a top-level `var FIXED_NEG_ONE = 0 - 65536;` or
`var FIXED_NEG_ONE = -65536;` — negative literals DO work in general
expressions now; it is only the enum-value grammar position that rejects
the leading `-`.

In cyrius-doom, converting constants to enums saved 30+ gvar_toks slots,
which made the difference between compiling and not compiling.

[[entries]]
name = "var_name_bracket_is_bytes_not_elements"
description = "`var name[N]` in a FUNCTION is N bytes (rounded up to 8); the SAME `var name[N]` at TOP LEVEL is N i64 SLOTS = N*8 bytes. Two different sizes for one syntax. Use `var a: i64[N]` (v6.2.1) to mean slots in both positions."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TWO MEANINGS — this is the part that bites hardest, and it is
NOT what the original note below said:

    fn f() {
        var buf[16];       # 16 BYTES (rounded up to a multiple of 8)
    }
    var buf[16];           # TOP LEVEL: 16 i64 SLOTS = 128 BYTES

Same three tokens, an 8× difference, decided by whether you are inside
a function. The top-level form was fixed to N*8 at v6.4.10 (it had been
inconsistent); the fn-local form has always been bytes.

WHERE THE TOP-LEVEL FORM BIT US: **CVE-34** (fixed v6.4.81). A
compiler-internal `var _cyrius_lib[256]` is 2048 bytes, not 256 — and
the code that copied into it had been reasoned about as if it were 256.
Reading a bare `var x[N]` and mentally assigning it N bytes at top
level is a live source of both over- and under-estimates.

THE FIX — SAY WHICH YOU MEAN (v6.2.1):

    var a: i64[N];     # N i64 slots = N*8 bytes — SAME in fn and top level
    var a: u8[N];      # N bytes explicitly — SAME in both
                       # widths i8..i64 / u8..u64, u128

The element width is registered into the var-size table, so every
backend (x86 / aarch64 / macho / pe / cx) inherits it. Prefer the
annotated form in new code; it removes the position dependence entirely.

THE ORIGINAL BITE (fn-local, still accurate):

    var buf[16];       # 16 bytes — OK for a 2-u64 pair
    var state[25];     # 25 BYTES — NOT 25 u64 lanes!
    var lanes[200];    # 200 bytes = 25 × u64 lanes

Surfaced v5.4.15 writing `lib/keccak.cyr` — `var state[25]` gave
25 bytes (should have been 200). First `memset(state, 0, 200)`
overwrote 175 bytes of adjacent stack locals including the
caller's return-address region. SIGSEGV on the first absorb.

RULE OF THUMB for the unannotated form: inside a fn, size it as
`N * 8` (or `N * sizeof(thing)`) yourself; at top level, do NOT — the
compiler already multiplied.

[[entries]]
name = "stack_var_keyword_for_struct_locals"
description = "v5.5.36 `stack var p: Point;` forces a named struct onto the frame; `var p: Point;` also now parses"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.5.36 added the `stack` storage-class keyword for named struct
locals, plus `var p: Point;` (no RHS) as a parse-level way to
declare a struct-typed local. Both allocate on the frame:

    fn demo() {
        stack var p: Point;       // explicit (preferred when
                                  // you want readers to notice)
        p.x = 1;
        p.y = 2;
    }

    fn demo2() {
        var q: Point;             // same effect, shorter form
        q.x = 3;
    }

`stack` is reserved (TOK=111). Variable and field names can't
use `stack` any more — rename to `stk`/`sp` if you collide.

Struct locals that previously needed anonymous filler `var`
slots to land at a known rbp disp can now just declare at the
struct type directly.

[[entries]]
name = "fn_main_is_not_the_entry_point"
description = "`fn main() { ... }` is just a function definition — cyrius has no auto-call for it; top-level statements are the program entry"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TRAP:

    fn main() {
        syscall(60, 42);
        return 0;
    }

Compiles clean. Runs. Exits with 0, or 1, or whatever argc was
— but never 42.

WHY:

Cyrius does not have a C-style `int main(void)` convention.
There is no implicit `bl _main` / `call main` emitted by the
compiler. The entry point is the top-level statements in the
compilation unit, emitted in order after all function bodies.
See `src/main.cyr` / `src/main_aarch64.cyr`:

    (pass 2) parse + emit every `fn` body into codebuf
    EPATCH(jmp_patch)            ← branch-over-fn-bodies lands here
    PARSE_ENUM_DEF(init pass)    ← enum init code
    EMIT_GVAR_INITS(S)           ← gvar init code
    PARSE_PROG(S)                ← YOUR top-level stmts run here
    EVLOAD(last_var); EEXIT(S)   ← exit with last-assigned var, or x0

A source consisting of only `fn main() { … }` has no top-level
code. After the parser finishes emitting `main`'s body as a
function (never called from anywhere), `PARSE_PROG` sees EOF
and emits nothing. The initial `b jmp_patch` that the entry
prologue emitted (to skip past the fn bodies) is then patched
to land directly on `EEXIT`, which exits with whatever was in
x0 / rax.

On Mach-O arm64 this is dramatic: the argv prologue pushes
x0 (argc) before the branch, so the `EEXIT` tail's
`movz x16,#1; svc #0x80` (= BSD `_exit(x0)`) exits with argc,
which is 1 when invoked from the shell.

CORRECT SHAPE:

    syscall(60, 42);

At top level. If you want helpers, define them as `fn`s and
call them from top level:

    fn add3(a, b, c) { return a + b + c; }
    syscall(60, add3(10, 20, 12));

WHERE THIS BIT US:

v5.6.33 was pinned as "macOS arm64 runtime regression" on
`ssh ecb` based on a `fn main()` fixture exiting 1 not 42.
There was no compiler regression; the fixture never exercised
what it thought it did. `otool -tv` showed `fn main`'s body
correctly emitting `adrp x16, 4; ldr x16, [x16]; br x16`
through `__got[0] = _exit` — it just never executed.

Linux has the same trap but argc is often 0 at process entry,
so the symptom looks like "exit 0" instead of "exit argc." Same
root cause; different flavor of wrong answer.

[[entries]]
name = "var_buf_in_library_functions"
description = "`var buf[N]` inside a library function is static data — every call shares the SAME buffer, and any Str borrowing from it dangles after the next call"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TRAP:

    fn parse_file(path) {
        var buf[262144];                       # NOT stack — static data
        var n = file_read_all(path, &buf, 262144);
        return parse(str_new(&buf, n));        # Str's data ptr → static buf
    }

This is the compounded form of "var buf[N] is BYTES not ELEMENTS."
NOTE (v6.3.15 default-on stack arrays): a fn-local `var buf[N]` now
behaves TWO ways by size — SMALL arrays route to per-thread STACK
slots (so a returned `&buf` DANGLES after the fn returns, reused by
the next call), while OVERSIZED arrays (over the per-fn stack budget,
as with `var buf[262144]`) STILL fall back to one shared global
buffer (the compiler emits `note: oversized array local kept in
shared global ... use alloc()` plus the `large static data` warning).
Either way the borrow is unsafe across calls. Sized properly, the
first call works; the second call clobbers the first — every Str the
first call returned now points at the second file's bytes (shared
global) or at reused stack (small).

You see this as parsed values bleeding across calls — IDs become
suffixes/prefixes of later IDs, descriptions become fragments of
the most-recently-read file. Nothing crashes; everything is just
wrong.

The build emits a hint:

    warning: large static data (262144 bytes) — consider alloc()
    for buffers >4K

Heed it. Library helpers that hand out heap pointers must heap-
allocate the backing memory:

    fn parse_file(path) {
        var buf = alloc(262144);               # fresh per call
        var n = file_read_all(path, buf, 262144);
        return parse(str_new(buf, n));
    }

Now the Str's data ptr aliases an alloc that lives until process
exit; consecutive calls keep their backing memory.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

vidya 2.3.0 — `lib/toml.cyr::toml_parse_file` shipped with
`var buf[262144]`. `vidya stats` segfaulted on the first
content-loading command after the cyrius 4.5.0 → 5.7.0 upgrade
because every concept's parsed Str pointed into the *last*
concept.toml's bytes; `concept_id` returned 0/garbage and
`path_join(0, fname)` faulted at NULL+offset. Workaround: read
the file into `alloc(N)` in the caller and call `toml_parse(...)`
directly, bypassing the leaky helper. Permanent fix belongs
upstream in cyrius stdlib.

This trap is invisible at single-call test scope. Every stdlib
helper that returns Str/buf-borrowing values must be exercised
with at least two consecutive calls in tests, otherwise the bug
sleeps until the second consumer arrives.

[[entries]]
name = "global_init_order_silent_zero"
description = "✅ FIXED in v5.7.32 (cyrlint warning) + v5.7.36 (string-literal awareness) — original bug behavior + workaround pattern preserved below for pre-v5.7.32 toolchain users"
category = "cyrius"
---
✅ FIXED in cyrius v5.7.32. `cyrlint`'s `lint_globals_init_order`
walks the file twice (pass 1 collects every top-level `var IDENT =
...;` with line, pass 2 walks every `var X = expr;` initializer
and emits a warning if any IDENT in the expr was declared at a
line > X's line). v5.7.36 added string-literal awareness so
identifier-shaped substrings inside `"..."` literals don't
false-positive. Tracked at:
`cyrius/docs/development/issues/archived/2026-04-28-global-init-order-forward-ref.md`
(v5.7.32 fix; archived 2026-05-03 during the v5.8.41 cyrlint
cleanup pass). Body below preserved as the v5.8.47 vidya audit
cross-referenced this entry as the canonical example of the
"description was stale; body had the correct upstream status"
pattern.

PRE-v5.7.32 BUG (kept for historical context):

Cyrius initializes top-level `var X = expr;` declarations in source
declaration order. If `expr` references a constant declared LATER in
the same file, that reference resolves to `0` (the default
zero-initialized value) at the time `X` is evaluated. No warning,
no error — `X` ends up holding the wrong value, and downstream
consumers silently see the wrong value too.

    # In src/foo.cyr (single file, declaration order matters):

    var COMPUTED = FLAG_A | FLAG_B | FLAG_C;   # → 0, NOT 7

    # ... 200 lines later ...

    var FLAG_A = 0x1;
    var FLAG_B = 0x2;
    var FLAG_C = 0x4;

    # COMPUTED reads as 0 throughout the program.

A trivial assertion surfaces it instantly:

    fn test() {
        assert_eq(COMPUTED, FLAG_A | FLAG_B | FLAG_C, "computed = OR");
        # → fails with "got 0, expected 7"
    }

WHERE THIS BIT US:

mabda v3 Step 4f.ii (BO page-perms tightening). Added named
perm-bitmask constants near the top of `src/backend_native.cyr`:

    var _NATIVE_PERM_FULL = AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_READABLE
                          | AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_WRITEABLE
                          | AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_EXECUTABLE;

at line 117. The `AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_*` constants lived at line 391+ in
the same file. Result: `_NATIVE_PERM_FULL` evaluated to `0` at load
time. Every BO got mapped with `perms = 0` — no read, no write, no
execute. Every dispatch TDR'd at the 10-second AMDGPU timeout with
"post-dispatch marker stale" + "output unchanged."

Visible failure mode looked like a wedged GPU — ~30 minutes spent
on hardware-iteration hypotheses (firmware near-permanent-wedge
rule, ring corruption, bad VAs) before a CPU regression test
`assert_eq(_NATIVE_PERM_FULL, R|W|X)` returned `got 0, expected 14`
and pinned the actual cause.

Fix: one-line move. `_NATIVE_PERM_*` block relocated below the
`AMDGPU_VM_PAGE_*` defs. Code that was "wrong since the moment I
typed it" started working immediately on hardware.

WORKAROUND PATTERN until the lint lands:

  1. For computed-from-other-constants globals, place the
     definition AFTER its dependencies (forced reordering).
  2. Add CPU regression tests that assert the *value* of every
     computed perm/flag/size constant. These are 5-line tests
     that catch this class of bug before any hardware
     iteration.
  3. When refactoring top-level declarations, re-run the value
     assertions. Reordering is a top-level change with non-local
     consequences in Cyrius.

UPSTREAM STATUS:

**✅ Shipped at v5.7.32 + v5.7.36** — picked option (a):
`cyrlint` rule `lint_globals_init_order` walks the file
twice. Pass 1 collects every top-level `var IDENT = ...;`
and records `(name, line)`. Pass 2 walks every initializer
expression, scans IDENT tokens, and emits a warning if any
IDENT was declared at a line LATER than the current decl:

    warn line N: global var init refs 'NAME' declared at line M
                 (silent zero at init)

v5.7.36 extended the Pass-2 scanner with **string-literal
awareness** — IDENTs nested inside `"..."` strings or `'...'`
chars (with `\\` / `\"` / `\'` escapes) no longer trigger
spurious warnings. So `var MSG = "FLAG_LATER not yet
defined"; var FLAG_LATER = 1;` is clean.

The compile-time error path (option b) was rejected because
existing stdlib shapes rely on declaration order in benign
ways; promoting to error would break green builds across
the ecosystem. Lint warning is the right level — surfaces
the foot-gun without forcing churn.

Affects ANY computed-from-other-constants global: size
calculations, page perms, bit-packed enum compositions,
refactors that reorder top-level declarations, the common
"define module constants near the top of the file for grep-
ability" pattern.

[[entries]]
name = "bump_allocator_exhaustion_in_tests"
description = "The stdlib bump allocator never frees during process lifetime — but since v6.1.19 it CHAINS fresh 256 MB chunks instead of failing, so the DOOM/mabda-era 'exhausted at the 9th alloc(112)' symptom is retired. alloc() returns 0 only for size<=0, size>ALLOC_MAX (2 GiB), or a failed mmap. The no-free property is still real; use an arena when you need to reclaim."
category = "cyrius"
---
⚠ **CORRECTED 2026-08-07 (v6.5.10), by running it.** The MECHANISM
below is out of date and the numbers with it. `lib/alloc.cyr` has
been a CHUNKED bump allocator since **v6.1.19**: it bumps inside the
current chunk and, on overflow, `mmap`s a FRESH 256 MB chunk
(`_LINUX_CHUNK = 0x10000000`) and continues there. Old chunks stay
mapped and valid; a single allocation never spans two. Measured on
this box: 4 × `alloc(100000000)` = 400 MB, crossing the first chunk,
all non-zero.

So today `alloc(N)` returns 0 in exactly three cases:

    N <= 0                       # rejected
    N  > ALLOC_MAX (0x80000000)  # 2 GiB single-allocation cap
    the mmap for a new chunk failed

"Ran out of heap after nine 112-byte contexts" is no longer reachable.
If you are chasing a null from `alloc` today, check the SIZE first —
a negative or overflowed size argument is the live cause, not
cumulative pressure.

⛔ **What IS still true, and is the reason to keep reading:** nothing
is ever freed. `alloc` has no `free`. A long-running process that
allocates per request grows without bound, and the only batch release
is `alloc_reset()`, which **invalidates EVERY pointer the allocator
has ever handed out** — including ones the stdlib itself is holding.
Any `Str`, `Vec`, `HashMap`, arena or struct built before the reset is
dangling afterwards, and the span is zeroed AND re-issuable, so a
stale pointer reads zeros until something else is allocated over it
and then reads that. v6.5.7 fixed one instance of this *inside the
stdlib* — `default_alloc()` memoized its vtable in the very arena it
describes, so `alloc_reset()` invalidated the allocator itself and
every later `default_alloc()` returned a dangling pointer
(reproducible SIGSEGV, hisab-filed). The vtable now lives in a
dedicated `_default_allocator_storage` global outside any resettable
region. **Consumer-held pointers are still the caller's problem.**
Reset only when nothing from the previous epoch will be read again.

For per-request memory you actually want reclaimed, use an ARENA
(`arena_new` / `arena_new_growable` / `arena_reset`) rather than
leaning on the global bump — and read
`arena_exhaustion_is_silent_because_a_zero_str_looks_valid` in this
file before you size one.

ORIGINAL (DOOM/mabda-era, kept for historical context — the WRITE
IDIOM at the bottom is still good advice, the diagnosis is not):

The Cyrius stdlib `alloc` is a bump allocator: each `alloc(N)`
moves a global pointer forward by N bytes; allocations are
NEVER freed during process lifetime. This is fine for short-
running programs (test suites, benchmarks, smoke programs) but
breaks down when a single process accumulates enough
allocations to exhaust the underlying heap segment.

**Symptom.** `alloc(N)` silently returns 0. Subsequent `store*`
to the returned pointer dereferences null → segfault. The
crash looks like an unrelated bug in test code that seemed to
work before.

**Where it bites.** Test files that grow over time and run all
their tests in one `main()` invocation. Each test that calls
`alloc(N)` permanently consumes that N. After enough tests,
the cumulative consumption exceeds the heap segment.

**Diagnostic.** If a test fails with exit 139 (SIGSEGV) and
inserting `assert(ctx != 0, "alloc returned non-null")`
catches it, the bump allocator is exhausted. mabda hit this
during Step 7.7 — adding the 9th `alloc(112)` ctx in
`tests/tcyr/mabda_v3_phase_d.tcyr` made `alloc` start
returning 0.

**The fix.** Stack-local arrays for test-scoped buffers:

```cyrius
# Before — heap-allocated, accumulates:
fn test_thing() {
    var ctx = alloc(112);
    memset(ctx, 0, 112);
    # ...
}

# After — stack-allocated, freed at fn return:
fn test_thing() {
    var ctx[112];
    memset(&ctx, 0, 112);
    # ...
}
```

The `var name[N]` form allocates on the stack, gets reclaimed
at fn exit. Identical observable behavior for test code; zero
heap pressure.

**When you MUST use alloc.** When a value needs to outlive the
fn (heap-allocated structs returned from constructors,
caller-owned buffers passed across abstraction boundaries).
Production code generally needs alloc; test code generally
doesn't.

**Why not fix the bump allocator.** The bump-allocator design
is intentional — its simplicity is the load-bearing property
for AGNOS's "no GC, no malloc" stance. Adding a heap-replacing
allocator would change the contract for every consumer.
Better to teach the test-write idiom: stack-local for
test-scoped buffers.

**Cross-pollination.** Every AGNOS test suite hits this
eventually. Teach the pattern at the start; don't wait for
the segfault. samvada's `tests/samvada.tcyr` already uses
stack-local idiom; mabda's older test files retrofitted as
they grew.

[[entries]]
name = "unwrap_is_option_only_result_silently_corrupts"
description = "unwrap()/unwrap_or() operate on Option (Some/None); on a Result they silently return the raw error code instead of aborting — use result_unwrap/is_ok/payload for Results"
category = "cyrius"
---
`unwrap(opt)` (lib/tagged.cyr) checks ONLY `is_none()` (tag == None == 0) before
returning `payload(opt)`. A `Result` (lib/result.cyr) has Ok tag=0, Err tag=1 —
tag layouts that OVERLAP Option's None=0/Some=1. So calling `unwrap()` on a Result
is a category error with NO diagnostic:

- an **Err** (tag=1, != None) sails past the `is_none` check → `unwrap` hands back
  `payload(res)` = the RAW ERROR CODE as if it were a valid value (e.g. a negative
  fd fed straight into a syscall);
- an **Ok** (tag=0 == None) makes `unwrap` ABORT with "unwrap called on None".

Because the tags overlap, the mismatch never faults at the unwrap site — it
CORRUPTS DOWNSTREAM. This bit the async net client: `lib/net.cyr` fns
(`tcp_socket`, `sock_accept`, `sock_connect`, `sock_send/recv`) return a **Result**,
but the reactor glue reached for the familiar `unwrap()`. For Results use
`is_ok(res)` / `is_err_result(res)` then `payload(res)`, or `result_unwrap()` /
`result_unwrap_or()`. Rule of thumb: `unwrap` is Option-only; if a fn returns
`Result<T,E>`, never `unwrap` it.


[[entries]]
name = "sockaddr_ipv4_is_network_order_bytes_packed_as_int"
description = "async_connect/async_resolve pass IPv4 as the 4 network-order octets packed low-byte-first into an int (127.0.0.1 = 0x0100007F), NOT the host-order a.b.c.d value; resolve→connect round-trips untouched"
category = "cyrius"
---
The `addr` argument to `async_connect(rt, addr, port)` (and the nameserver addr to
`async_resolve`) is the four IPv4 octets in **network byte order, packed low-byte-
first into an integer** — e.g. 127.0.0.1 is `0x0100007F` (b0=127 in the LOW byte),
matching how it is stored raw with `store32(&sa+4, addr)` into `sockaddr_in`. The
`port` is HOST order and byte-swapped inline to network order at the store site.
`async_resolve` returns its resolved A record in the IDENTICAL packed form
(`_async_dns_parse_a` reassembles the 4 answer octets as
`b0 | (b1<<8) | (b2<<16) | (b3<<24)`), so a resolve result feeds STRAIGHT into
`async_connect` with no conversion. The trap: this int is NOT the human-readable
a.b.c.d and NOT host-order — passing `0x7F000001` (host-order 127.0.0.1), or
`htonl`-ing the resolve output before connect, silently connects to the wrong host
with no error. Same convention across net.cyr's sockaddr builders.

[[entries]]
name = "string_literal_to_str_coercion_is_annotation_only"
description = "A bare `\"...\"` coerces to a `Str` struct ONLY when the receiving param is annotated `: Str`. Into an UNTYPED param it arrives as a raw cstr pointer, and any str_* call then reads `load64(ptr+8)` as garbage — a silent empty/zero result, never an error. Shipped undetected for ~a year across 11 lib/fs.cyr fns; fixed v6.4.72."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE RULE: the compiler's string-literal → `Str` auto-coercion
(`parse_fn.cyr`) fires only when BOTH hold —

    1. the argument is a LITERAL token, and
    2. the receiving parameter is annotated `: Str`.

Miss either and you get the raw null-terminated `cstr` pointer.

THE SILENT FAILURE:

    fn find_files(path, ext) {          # UNTYPED params
        ...
        if (str_ends_with(name, ext)) { ... }
    }

    find_files("src", ".cyr");          # returns 0 matches. No error.

  `".cyr"` enters as a cstr pointer, flows unchanged through the
  untyped `ext`, and reaches `str_ends_with`, whose `suffix: Str` IS
  typed — but coercion happens at the CALL BOUNDARY, and by then the
  value is an i64, not a literal token. `str_len(suffix)` does
  `load64(ptr+8)` and reads whatever sits 8 bytes past the string data.
  Every comparison fails. You get an empty result set, a zero count, a
  never-taken branch. **Never a diagnostic.**

WHY THE OBVIOUS TESTS PASS ANYWAY:

    str_ends_with(name, ".cyr")         # WORKS  — literal + `: Str` param
    find_files(path, str_from(".cyr"))  # WORKS  — real Str value
    find_files(path, ".cyr")            # BROKEN — literal + untyped param

  So a direct probe of the leaf function works, and any caller that
  already wrapped in `str_from` works. Only the bare-literal call into
  the untyped API is wrong, which is why it survived ~a year in
  `lib/fs.cyr` (`find_files`, `find_files_with_prunes`, `path_has_ext`
  and 8 siblings) and was found only when `cyrius coverage` reported
  `0/1097` and someone chased it. The working hypothesis at the time
  was allocator aliasing — it was not.

THE FIX IS AT THE API BOUNDARY, NOT THE CALL SITE:

    fn find_files(path: Str, ext: Str) { ... }   # annotate — done

  An `IDENT` argument is never coerced, so `str_from`-wrapped callers
  are byte-identical after the annotation. Annotating is free.

THIS IS NOT A COMPILER BUG. Literal-only coercion is deliberate: a
runtime `i64` cannot be known to be a `Str`. The library simply failed
to express its contract. The lesson is a review rule:

  **Any public fn that takes a string MUST annotate the param `: Str`.**
  An unannotated string param is an API defect, and it fails silently
  rather than loudly — which is the worst possible failure mode for a
  stdlib. Grep your own modules for `fn <name>(...)` with a
  string-shaped parameter and no `: Str`.

CROSS-REF: CHANGELOG [6.4.72]; the same release fixed `cyrius coverage`,
which had been working AROUND this bug with an inline `dir_walk` +
`str_ends_with` filter.

[[entries]]
name = "str_int_cstr_are_reserved_fn_name_suffixes_that_rewrite_your_call_sites"
description = "**`_str`, `_int` and `_cstr` are RESERVED FUNCTION-NAME SUFFIXES in cyrius, not free-form naming.** If `X` and `X_str` both exist, the compiler REWRITES a call `X(a, ...)` into `X_str(a, ...)` whenever argument 1 is `Str`-typed — likewise `X_int` for an i64-returning call argument. Registration is bidirectional at `REGFN`, so include order does not save you. The mechanism exists for genuine type variants (`println(cstr)` / `println_str(Str)`) and it is invisible until it misfires. **THE RULE: `X_str` must be the Str-taking variant of `X` — a cstr+len form may NEVER occupy that name.** Violating it means calls to `X` silently execute `X_str`. This is not hypothetical: bayan named its cstr+len entries `bayan_json_v_parse_str(buf, len)` alongside `bayan_json_v_parse(src)`, so every `bayan_json_v_parse(someStr)` in the ecosystem became a **1-argument call to a 2-argument function** — `len` bound to garbage, parse returned 0 for every input including valid documents — and the only outward sign was one `expects 2 arguments, got 1` warning line lost in build output. It cost bayan a BREAKING public-API rename (1.3.0, `_str` -> `_buf`) to escape, and sakshi carried the same collision latently (`_sk_write_str/5` shadowing the unrelated `_sk_write/2`). **As of cyrius 6.5.1 the compiler is arity-aware** — it only redirects when the target's parameter count matches the arguments you actually supplied — and a wrong argument count is now a hard ERROR that withholds the binary rather than a warning. **`_int` IS DIFFERENT FROM `_str`, and as of 6.5.2 it has an explicit condition.** The `_int` route only ever fired for a bare FN-CALL first argument — never a literal, never a variable — so `take(v)` and `take(42)` called the base while `take(mk())` ran `take_int`: the same value dispatching differently depending on whether it had passed through a variable. From 6.5.2 the route requires the base's parameter 0 to be explicitly annotated **`: cstring`**, which is the condition it was always approximating (\"this param wants a POINTER, so convert the number for it\"). Practical rule: if you want `X`/`X_int` auto-dispatch, annotate X's first parameter `: cstring`; if you do NOT want it, leave it unannotated and the pair is inert. **Also 6.5.2: passing an integer LITERAL to a `: cstring` parameter is now a hard error** — it used to compile silently and SIGSEGV, since the callee dereferences the number as a `char*` (`println(42)` printed nothing and died on signal 11). `0` remains legal as NULL. An i64 VARIABLE or a fn-call result passed to a `: cstring` param is still only a warning, and always will be: under ADR-002's i64-everywhere tenet those are genuinely indistinguishable from a pointer. A literal is the one case the compiler can actually judge. So the trap is defused on 6.5.1+, but the NAMING rule still stands: pick `_buf` (or anything that is not `_str` / `_int` / `_cstr`) for a pointer+length form, because a suffix that lies about a function's type is confusing even when it is no longer dangerous. See CHANGELOG [6.5.1], bayan CHANGELOG [1.3.0]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "async_await_readable_ms_bounded_wait"
description = "`async_await_readable(fd)` parks on epoll with a hardcoded -1 and returns a constant 0 — a cooperative server parked there cannot be woken to STOP. Use `async_await_readable_ms(fd, ms)` (v6.5.6): 1 = readable, 0 = timed out."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.6** — filed by sandhi/agnosai while building a graceful-shutdown facility. `async_await_readable(fd)` builds its own epfd, registers `fd`, and calls `sys_epoll_wait(epfd, &revents, 1, 0 - 1)`. Two separate problems live in that one line:

1. **The `-1` is unbounded.** A caller parked there is unwakeable by anything except data on `fd` — no flag, no deadline, no cancellation. Fine for a server whose only job is to serve; fatal for one that must also **stop**. An idle accept loop never observes a shutdown flag set by another thread, because control never leaves the call. A signal handler can fire perfectly and change nothing.
2. **The return value was discarded.** The helper returned a constant `0` regardless of outcome, so even *with* a timeout a caller could not distinguish "the fd is readable" from "the wait expired". A bounded wait that cannot report which happened is not usable.

`async_await_readable_ms(fd, ms)` fixes both: **1** = readable, **0** = timed out, negative `ms` blocks forever. `async_await_readable` remains as a wrapper returning `0`, so no existing call site moves.

WHAT DOESN'T COVER THIS. `async_with_timeout(rt, handle, ms)` looks like the answer and is not — it races a *spawned task* against a deadline sentinel inside a runtime, whereas this is a bare fd-readiness wait taken on the accept path, outside any task, before a runtime is meaningfully in play.

EINTR. A signal-interrupted wait reports `0`, the same as a timeout. That is deliberate: the caller's loop re-checks its own condition and waits again, which is the correct response to both.

SCOPE. `lib/async.cyr`'s epoll body is gated NOT-agnos AND NOT-win, so this pair is Linux-path only by construction (Windows has `lib/async_win.cyr`, agnos `lib/async_agnos.cyr`). On the **cx** target no syscall peer is included at all, so the body does not even compile there — no cross-target work is owed.

THE TESTING LESSON. Both directions need pinning. A variant hardwired to `1` passes an "it returned instead of hanging" test; a variant hardwired to `0` — the *old* contract — passes that one too. `tests/async_await_readable_ms.sh` asserts an idle fd reports 0 **and** a readable fd reports 1; each mutation fails exactly one axis.

[[entries]]
name = "i64_min_negation_is_a_noop_and_division_truncates_toward_zero"
description = "`n = 0 - n` is a NO-OP at i64::MIN (two's-complement abs overflows back to itself), so `n == 0` and `n > 0` are BOTH false and any `while (n > 0)` digit loop runs zero times. That is why every decimal formatter in the stdlib emitted a bare `-` for i64::MIN until v6.5.8. The fix is a sign FLAG + a `!= 0` loop + per-digit negation — which is safe because `/` and `%` truncate toward zero on x86, aarch64 AND cx."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.8** — 12 sites, not the 7 the roadmap named.

THE ARITHMETIC (all four probed by running the compiler at v6.5.10):

    var m  = 1 << 63;        # i64::MIN
    0 - m  == m              # TRUE  — negation is a NO-OP here
    m > 0                    # FALSE — so a `while (n > 0)` loop never runs
    (0 - 7) / 2 == 0 - 3     # TRUE  — truncates TOWARD ZERO, not floor
    (0 - 7) % 2 == 0 - 1     # TRUE  — remainder takes the DIVIDEND's sign

i64 is two's complement, so the magnitude of `i64::MIN` is one larger
than `i64::MAX` and has no positive representation. `0 - n` overflows
straight back to `n`. There is no error, no trap, no diagnostic.

THE SHAPE THAT BREAKS. Every hand-rolled decimal formatter is written
this way, in every language:

    if (n < 0) { write('-'); n = 0 - n; }    # ← NO-OP at i64::MIN
    if (n == 0) { write('0'); }              # ← skipped, n is not 0
    while (n > 0) { ... }                    # ← skipped, n is not > 0

At `i64::MIN` the sign byte is written and then NOTHING. `fmt_int`
printed `-`. `str_from_int(i64::MIN)` was the one-character string
`"-"`, and bayan then serialised `{"n":-}` — not JSON — with no error
raised at any layer.

THE FIX — keep the sign as a FLAG, never negate `n`:

    var neg: i64 = 0;
    if (n < 0) { neg = 1; }
    if (n == 0) { store8(&buf + bi, 48); bi = bi - 1; }
    while (n != 0) {                     # `!= 0`, NOT `> 0`
        var d = n % 10;
        if (d < 0) { d = 0 - d; }        # negate the DIGIT, not n
        store8(&buf + bi, 48 + d); bi = bi - 1; n = n / 10;
    }
    if (neg == 1) { store8(&buf + bi, 45); bi = bi - 1; }

`!= 0` is the load-bearing half. Extracting digits from a negative
value only works because `/` and `%` truncate toward zero on **all
three** cyrius backends — that was verified on x86_64, aarch64 and cx
before the fix relied on it, not assumed. (Under floor division
`(0-7)/2` would be `-4` and the digits would come out wrong.)

WHERE IT WAS. Four in `lib/fmt.cyr` (`fmt_int`, `fmt_int_fd`,
`fmt_int_buf`, and a sibling), plus `lib/string.cyr`, `lib/log.cyr`,
sakshi (fixed upstream at 2.4.8 and re-vendored), **two inside the
compiler itself** (`PRNUM`, `_emit_decimal`) that no roadmap entry
listed, and three shipped demos.

⭐ **THE LESSON IS THE HALF-FIX.** `fmt_hex`, sitting directly below
`fmt_int` in the same file, had already been corrected for this exact
`> 0` vs `!= 0` reason at **v6.4.69**. The decimal siblings were never
brought along. When you fix a loop-bound predicate in one formatter,
grep the file for every other `while (n > 0)` before you close it.

REVIEW RULE for your own code: any `while (x > 0)` over an i64 that
came from user data is suspect, and any `x = 0 - x` "take the absolute
value" is a bug at exactly one input. Test formatters and parsers at
`1 << 63`, not just at `-1`.

[[entries]]
name = "arena_exhaustion_is_silent_because_a_zero_str_looks_valid"
description = "An exhausted arena returns 0 from `arena_alloc` / `str_from_a` — correct behaviour — but a `Str` of 0 is INDISTINGUISHABLE from a valid `Str`, there is no option type and no error channel through the `_a` families, so it flows on and faults layers away. v6.5.9 adds a per-arena exhaustion POLICY (ARENA_FULL_NULL / GROW / SPILL / ABORT) precisely because returning 0 correctly was never enough."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.9** — filed from agnosai.

THE PRIMITIVES WERE ALREADY RIGHT, AND THAT WAS THE PROBLEM.
`arena_alloc` returns 0 when the chunk cannot serve the request,
`str_from_a` returns 0, `vec_push_a` returns -1. None of that is the
bug. The bug is what the 0 does next: **a `Str` of 0 is
indistinguishable from a valid `Str`** — there is no option type, and
the `_a` (allocator-threaded) families have no error channel — so it
flows onward and the first thing that dereferences it faults. "Arena
too small" was observable as a SIGSEGV several layers away, in a JSON
walker, with no indication which allocator ran out.

⭐ **THE REGRESSION DIRECTION IS WHAT MAKES IT SERIOUS.** Before
threading a route onto an arena, an oversized response was a *leak* on
the no-free global bump. After threading — a change whose entire
purpose is to stop leaking — the same response is a *crash*. Adopting
the feature correctly made the failure worse and quieter. A
5,500-assertion suite missed it because allocation tests are
small-fixture by nature: they assert `bytes == 0` over a handful of
items, and nothing about that shape probes the ceiling.

THE POLICY (one field on the arena header, set at creation or later):

    ARENA_FULL_NULL  = 0    return 0 — TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR, still the
                            default, so no existing arena moves
    ARENA_FULL_GROW  = 1    chain another chunk
    ARENA_FULL_SPILL = 2    serve overflow from the global allocator
                            (weaker — spilled bytes are never reclaimed
                            by reset — but the crash becomes the
                            pre-arena cost)
    ARENA_FULL_ABORT = 3    die loudly AT the allocation instead of
                            faulting three layers away

    var a  = arena_new_growable(65536);        # policy = GROW
    var al = arena_allocator_growable(65536);  # the allocator-interface form
    arena_set_on_full(a, ARENA_FULL_ABORT);    # or change it later
    arena_on_full(a)                           # read it back, -1 if a == 0

⚠ **GROW RETAINS ITS CHUNKS ACROSS `arena_reset`.** The bump allocator
underneath has no `free()`, so releasing them is not expressible — and
it is not what an arena wants anyway. Reset rewinds to the FIRST chunk
and re-uses the chain, so a request loop converges on its high-water
mark and then allocates nothing. Use `arena_capacity_total(a)` for the
whole chain; `arena_used(a)` cannot show it once the arena has grown.

⚠ `arena_free(a)` used to clear only base/ptr/end and LEAVE THE POLICY
SET, so a "freed" growable arena would take the grow path and succeed.
Fixed in the same release — but if you are on an older toolchain, do
not reuse a freed arena handle.

PRACTICAL RULE: if you cannot bound the allocation from the input,
either use `arena_new_growable` and stop guessing a capacity, or set
`ARENA_FULL_ABORT` so a wrong guess is a diagnosable death rather than
a mystery segfault in whatever code touched the pointer next. Sizing an
arena and hoping is the shape this entry exists to stop.

[[entries]]
name = "thread_is_done_is_valid_only_before_thread_join"
description = "`thread_is_done(t)` (v6.5.8) is a non-blocking poll of the handle `thread_create` returned — valid ONLY BEFORE `thread_join`, because join CONSUMES the handle (on Windows it CloseHandle()s it) and a post-join query reports \"not done\" forever. A detached thread has no handle at all, by design."
category = "cyrius"
---
**v6.5.8** — the reaper primitives.

    var t = thread_create(&worker, arg);
    while (thread_is_done(t) == 0) { ... keep serving ... }
    thread_join(t);                     # then join once — and STOP asking

`thread_join` blocks unconditionally, which made a reaper — "collect
whichever workers have finished, keep serving the rest" — impossible to
write without a detached thread plus a side channel. `thread_is_done`
reads the same `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` slot `thread_join`'s loop already
tests, so it adds no new state: **1** = done, **0** = still running,
**-1** = null handle.

⛔ **VALID ONLY BEFORE `thread_join`.** Join CONSUMES the handle — on
Windows it `CloseHandle()`s it — so a post-join query inspects a closed
handle and reports "not done", forever. That is not a bug to work
around; after joining, the thread is finished by definition and there is
nothing left to ask. Caught on real cass hardware, by a test that
asserted the wrong contract.

⛔ **A DETACHED THREAD HAS NO HANDLE.** `thread_create_detached(fp,
arg)` returns 1/0 (spawned or not), not a handle, and there is nothing
to poll or join — the child unmaps its own stack in the trampoline tail
and the TLS block is carved from the top of that same mapping so one
`munmap` frees both. Use it for fire-and-forget work; use
`thread_create` + `thread_is_done` + `thread_join` when you need to know
it finished. (Before .8 the only fire-and-forget shape was an unjoined
`thread_create`, which leaked **2,101,248 bytes of VA per thread** —
100 unjoined threads = 210,124,800 B, now 0.)

⚠ RELATED, SAME RELEASE: `thread_join` itself had a lost-wakeup
deadlock — it read the tid word TWICE per iteration, once for the loop
condition and once as the `FUTEX_WAIT` expected value, so a worker
exiting between the two loads let the kernel's one-shot wake fire with
nobody parked and the joiner then parked on an address that would never
be woken again. Permanent, silent, log-less, ~1 join in 150k, SIGKILL
the only recovery. Fixed in v6.5.8. If you are on ≤6.5.7 and a
long-running server wedges in a join, this is it.
